---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { implementationDoc } from '../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="sempods use cases — public knowledge, services and agent memory"
  description="See live event data and explore services, personal memory and shared knowledge built around a pod the owner controls."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">what it enables</p>
  <h1>Let tools cooperate around the data</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A useful app does not have to become the permanent home of your
    knowledge. Give the data its own address, meaning and permissions,
    and different tools can work with it.
  </p>
  <section class="use-case">
    <h2>Events as Linked Open Data</h2>
    <p>
      The aaltra venue publishes real events from its pod. A website can
      render those events, a calendar can read their dates, and an agent can
      query them. Each client uses the same linked source data.
    </p>
    <p class="evidence">
      This is live: <a href="/start#query">run the public query</a> and follow
      one of the returned event addresses.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="use-case">
    <h2>A worker with authority the owner chooses</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine a notes service that registers on your pod. You choose the
      Contexts it may reach. It can then work while you are offline, using
      its own credentials, and you can narrow or revoke its grants.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kotlin 0.2 supplies experimental registration and owner-consent flows
      for this pattern. The <a href={implementationDoc('sempods-server/docs/auth/service-clients.md')}>service-access guide</a>{' '}shows the working setup; the notes service is an example use case.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="use-case">
    <h2>Personal memory that can outlast the assistant</h2>
    <p>
      Notes, plans, projects and observations could become a graph in your
      pod. An agent could find relevant resources and follow links while
      another app helps you browse or edit the same knowledge.
    </p>
    <p>
      MCP and the data operations supply building blocks today. A complete
      personal memory experience is a direction to explore, not a packaged
      product delivered by the Kotlin release.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="use-case">
    <h2>Shared knowledge for teams and communities</h2>
    <p>
      A museum could publish a collection's public facts and keep working
      notes private. A team could give support tools access to product
      knowledge while holding internal plans back.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are scenarios for the same model: shared meaning, explicit
      authority and links that can cross data spaces. The applications and
      their operating arrangements still need to be built and tested.
    </p>
  </section>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="/start#build">Build a client</a>{' '}
    <a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Explore retrieval</a>{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/discussions">Discuss a use case</a>
  </p>
</Base>
